n\\}\\), which we denote by \\(R(g,n)\\). For example, we prove that \\(R(g,n) < 1.31\\sqrt{gn}\\) for all \\(n \\ge g \\ge 2\\), while \\(R(g,n) > 0.79\\sqrt{gn}\\) for sufficiently large integers \\(g\\) and \\(n\\).\n\nThese two problems are so interconnected that both continuous and discrete tools can be applied to each problem with surprising effectiveness. The harmonic analysis methods and inequalities among various \\(L^p\\) norms we use to derive lower bounds for \\(\\Delta(\\epsilon)\\) also provide uniform upper bounds for \\(R(g,n)\\), while the techniques from combinatorial and probabilistic number theory that we employ to obtain constructions of large \\(B^*[g]\\) sets yield strong upper bounds for \\(\\Delta(\\epsilon)\\).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContinuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets (https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0210041)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.1 years ago; Title: Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets ... A set S of integers is called a B*[g] set if for any given m there are at most g ordered pairs (s_1,s_2) \\in S \\times S with s_1+s_2=m; in the case g=2, these are better known as Sidon sets.\nTitle: Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets\nAuthors: Greg Martin, Kevin O'Bryant\nDate: Thu Oct  3 02:22:08 2002\n\nA symmetric subset of the reals is one that remains invariant under some reflection x --> c-x. Given 0 < x < 1, there exists a real number D(x) with the following property: if 0 < d < D(x), then every subset of [0,1] with measure x contains a symmetric subset with measure d, while if d > D(x), then there exists a subset of [0,1] with measure x that does not contain a symmetric subset with measure d. In this paper we establish upper and lower bounds for D(x) of the same order of magnitude: for example, we prove that D(x) = 2x - 1 for 11/16 < x < 1 and that 0.59 x^2 < D(x) < 0.8 x^2 for 0 < x < 11/16. This continuous problem is intimately connected with a corresponding discrete problem. A set S of integers is called a B*[g] set if for any given m there are at most g ordered pairs (s_1,s_2) \\in S \\times S with s_1+s_2=m; in the case g=2, these are better known as Sidon sets. We also establish upper and lower bounds of the same order of magnitude for the maximal possible size of a B*[g] set contained in {1,...,n}, which we denote by R(g,n). For example, we prove that R(g,n) < 1.31 \\sqrt{gn} for all n > g > 1, while R(g,n) > 0.79 \\sqrt{gn} for sufficiently large integers g and n. These two problems are so interconnected that both continuous and discrete tools can be applied to each problem with surprising effectiveness. The harmonic analysis methods and inequalities among various L^p norms we use to derive lower bounds for D(x) also provide uniform upper bounds for R(g,n), while the techniques from combinatorial and probabilistic number theory that we employ to obtain constructions of large B*[g] sets yield strong upper bounds for D(x).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nContinuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/math/0210041v2)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 24.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets ... A set S of integers is called a B*[g] set if for any given m there are at most g ordered pairs (s_1,s_2) \\in S \\times S with s_1+s_2=m; in the case g=2, these are better known as Sidon sets.\n\n## Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets\n\nGreg Martin, Kevin O'Bryant\n\nPublished: 2002-10-03 | Updated: 2002-10-05\n\nComments: 66 pages, 8 figures, 3 tables; formatting improved\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nramsey theory\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nA symmetric subset of the reals is one that remains invariant under some reflection x --> c-x. Given 0 < x < 1, there exists a real number D(x) with the following property: if 0 < d < D(x), then every subset of [0,1] with measure x contains a symmetric subset with measure d, while if d > D(x), then there exists a subset of [0,1] with measure x that does not contain a symmetric subset with measure d. In this paper we establish upper and lower bounds for D(x) of the same order of magnitude: for example, we prove that D(x) = 2x - 1 for 11/16 < x < 1 and that 0.59 x^2 < D(x) < 0.8 x^2 for 0 < x < 11/16. This continuous problem is intimately connected with a corresponding discrete problem. A set S of integers is called a B*[g] set if for any given m there are at most g ordered pairs (s_1,s_2) \\in S \\times S with s_1+s_2=m; in the case g=2, these are better known as Sidon sets. We also establish upper and lower bounds of the same order of magnitude for the maximal possible size of a B*[g] set contained in {1,...,n}, which we denote by R(g,n). For example, we prove that R(g,n) < 1.31 \\sqrt{gn} for all n > g > 1, while R(g,n) > 0.79 \\sqrt{gn} for sufficiently large integers g and n. These two problems are so interconnected that both continuous and discrete tools can be applied to each problem with surprising effectiveness. The harmonic analysis methods and inequalities among various L^p norms we use to derive lower bounds for D(x) also provide uniform upper bounds for R(g,n), while the techniques from combinatorial and probabilistic number theory that we employ to obtain constructions of large B*[g] sets yield strong upper bounds for D(x).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAuthor’s abstract: “Let \\(S \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_m\\) be a Sidon set of cardinality \\(|S| = m^{1/2} + O(1)\\). It is proved, in particular, that for any interval \\(I = \\{a, a+1, \\ldots, a+\\ell-1\\}\\) in \\(\\mathbb{Z}_m\\), \\(0 \\le \\ell \\le m\\), we have \\(\\bigl||S \\cap I| - |S|\\ell/m\\bigr| = O(|S|^{1/2}\\log m)\\).” (https://www.combinatorics.org/ojs/index.php/eljc/article/download/DS11/pdf/)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.3 years ago; Author’s abstract: “Let \\(S \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_m\\) be a Sidon set of cardinality \\(|S| = m^{1/2} + O(1)\\). ... Our results, in particular, disprove a conjecture of Wood.” ... [126] Greg Martin and Kevin O’Bryant, *Continuous Ramsey theory and Sidon sets*, 2002, arXiv:math.NT/0210041.\nThe website http://www.math.cmu.edu/ pikhurko/Papers/index.html links to the preprint, which it notes as “to appear in Europ J. Comb.”\n\nAuthor’s abstract: “We label the vertices of a given graph \\(G\\) with positive integers so that the pairwise differences over its edges are all distinct. Let \\(\\mathcal{D}(G)\\) be the smallest value that the largest label can have.\n\nFor example, for the complete graph \\(K_n\\), the labels must form a Sidon set. Hence \\(\\mathcal{D}(K_n) = (1 + o(1))n^2\\). Rather surprisingly, we demonstrate that there are graphs with only \\(n^{3/2+o(1)}\\) edges achieving this bound.\n\nMore generally, we study the maximum value of \\(\\mathcal{D}(G)\\) that a graph \\(G\\) of the given order \\(n\\) and size \\(m\\) can have. We obtain bounds which are sharp up to a logarithmic multiplicative factor. The analogous problem for pairwise sums is considered as well. Our results, in particular, disprove a conjecture of Wood.”\n\n[125] M. Helm, *Upper bounds for \\(B_2[g]\\)-sets*, unpublished.\n\nI have not seen this article, but it reportedly contains a proof that \\(R(4,n) \\le \\sqrt{6n} + O(1)\\).\n\n[126] Greg Martin and Kevin O’Bryant, *Continuous Ramsey theory and Sidon sets*, 2002, arXiv:math.NT/0210041.\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS #DS11 37--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublication List of Kevin O'Bryant (https://www.math.csi.cuny.edu/~obryant/Mathematician/Papers/PapersList.html)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6.8 years ago; \"Continuous Ramsey Theory and Sidon Sets,\" (with Greg Martin) ... \"A Discrete Fourier Kernel and Fraenkel's Tili